-- rm-recurses-into-dot-and-dotdot: `rm -r .` fails only by accident of
-  `rmdir(".")`, and `rm -r ..` recursively deletes the *parent directory's
-  contents*. POSIX: if the last component of an operand is `.` or `..`, or
-  the operand resolves to `/`, `rm` writes a diagnostic and does nothing with
-  it. (Hit for real while auditing: it emptied a scratch directory.)
-
-  **Repro:**
-  ```sh
-  mkdir -p t/w; touch t/x; cd t/w; rm -r ..; ls ..   # empties t/ (w vanishes too)
-  rm -r .          # "rm: .: Invalid argument", nothing else refused up front
-  ```
-
